# SSO with KPMG — for engineers new to Azure

## A few terms first

- **Entra ID** — Microsoft's cloud identity service (used to be called Azure AD). It's where org accounts, groups, app permissions, and SSO live.
- **Tenant** — one organization's slice of Entra. Each company gets a tenant when they sign up for Microsoft 365 / Azure. It's the container for that org's users, groups, app registrations, and service principals. Normain has a tenant. KPMG has a tenant. They're isolated from each other unless explicitly federated.

## The setup

Two companies → two tenants:

- **Normain's tenant** — small, mostly exists to host the *app registration* (the definition of the Normain SaaS app).
- **KPMG Global tenant** — the giant one, holds ~250k KPMG employees worldwide.

When a KPMG employee logs into Normain, Normain has to verify their identity against KPMG's tenant. That requires a trust relationship between the two tenants.

## Two records have to exist before that trust works

| In Normain's tenant | In KPMG's tenant |
|---|---|
| **App registration** — "I'm an app called Normain, App ID `xyz`, here are the permissions I need, here's my redirect URI." | **Service principal** — "We know an outside app called Normain (App ID `xyz`). It's allowed to talk to us. These specific users can use it." |

Both records have to exist. They reference each other by Normain's App ID. Neither alone is enough.

- The Normain-side record is the **app registration**.
- The KPMG-side record is the **service principal** — same thing Microsoft calls an **enterprise application** in the Entra admin UI, same thing Digvijay called an **SPN** (loose carryover from on-prem AD terminology).

## The two ways to create the KPMG-side record

### The normal way — Microsoft's default consent flow

> A KPMG admin (a Global Admin in the KPMG Global tenant) clicks "Accept" on Normain's app once. Entra auto-creates the service principal in KPMG's tenant, pre-filled with whatever permissions Normain's app requested.

**The problem:** the "Accept" click is performed by a Global Admin against KPMG's *global* tenant — covering all 250k employees across 120+ member firms. KPMG Global has a blanket policy: no third-party apps get consented in the global directory. Ever. So this path is dead.

### KPMG's proposal — manual creation

> A KPMG admin opens the Entra portal and *manually creates* the service principal as a "non-gallery enterprise application." They configure SSO themselves (SAML or OIDC), set the user assignment list to only the relevant KPMG Sweden users, and hand Normain the federation details (app ID, issuer URL, signing cert / OIDC metadata).

Same end state — both records exist and reference each other — but every detail of the KPMG-side record is set by KPMG, not auto-filled by Microsoft from a generic "Accept" click. The trust is configured *on KPMG's terms*, scoped *by KPMG's assignment list*, and revocable *by KPMG alone*.

## So what is an "SPN" / service principal, really?

A service principal is an entry in KPMG's directory describing an external app they've agreed to let in. The entry holds:

- Which app it's about — the app's name (e.g. "Normain") and the unique ID Microsoft assigned to it
- Which KPMG users are allowed to use it
- What the app is allowed to ask KPMG about — typically only the identity of the person signing in, not the rest of the directory
- A log of every sign-in attempt
- Any extra rules KPMG wants to enforce (MFA, company laptop, etc.)

If the entry doesn't exist, KPMG's identity system won't issue credentials when someone tries to sign into Normain. Sign-in just fails — Normain's app never even sees the request.

A few things to be clear about:

- **The entry is just a record on KPMG's side.** Normain's code and infrastructure don't move; the app keeps running wherever Normain runs it.
- **KPMG owns the entry completely.** They can change permissions, remove users, or delete the entry. As soon as it's gone, Normain's access disappears — Normain has no way to put it back.
- **The disagreement with Digvijay is about who creates this entry and how.** In Microsoft's default flow, the entry is auto-created when KPMG's Global Admin clicks "Accept" — pre-filled with whatever Normain asked for. In KPMG's proposal, no Accept click happens; KPMG creates the entry by hand, deciding each detail themselves.

## What this argument was really about

Both flows produce the same two records linked by the same App ID. The fight was just **who writes the KPMG-side record, and how**:

- Microsoft's auto-pilot via admin consent → blocked by KPMG Global policy.
- KPMG's hand, via manual non-gallery SSO setup → allowed, but requires NITSO security sign-off.

## What Normain needs to deliver to KPMG

To let KPMG configure the SP correctly:

- The exact claims/permissions Normain's SaaS needs from the token (likely just `sub`, `email`, `preferred_username` — nothing requiring `Directory.Read.All`).
- Confirmation that Normain's app can federate against an externally-owned IdP app (i.e., accept KPMG's SP, not push Normain's app reg in).
- Protocol preference: SAML or OIDC. Non-gallery enterprise apps in Entra default to SAML; OIDC is also supported. Marketplace SaaS typically OIDC.
